To find the area of the land that is not watered, we first need to determine the area of the entire square plot and then subtract the area of the circular field from it. The side length of the square plot is 500 meters, so the area of the square is:

\begin{align*}
A_{\text{square}} &= s^2 \\
&= 500^2 \\
&= 250,000 \text{ square meters}
\end{align*}

To find the area of the circular field, we first need to determine the radius. Since the circle is inscribed in the square, its diameter is equal to the side length of the square. Therefore, the radius of the circle is half of the side length, which is:

\begin{align*}
r &= \frac{s}{2} \\
&= \frac{500}{2} \\
&= 250 \text{ meters}
\end{align*}

Now, we can use the formula for the area of a circle to find the area of the circular field:

\begin{align*}
A_{\text{circle}} &= \pi r^2 \\
&= \pi (250)^2 \\
&= 62,500 \pi \text{ square meters}
\end{align*}

Finally, to find the area of the land that is not watered, we subtract the area of the circular field from the area of the square plot:

\begin{align*}
A_{\text{not watered}} &= A_{\text{square}} - A_{\text{circle}} \\
&= 250,000 - 62,500 \pi \\
&\approx 80,467 \text{ square meters (to the nearest thousand)}
\end{align*}

Therefore, the area of the land that is not watered is approximately 80,000 square meters.
